record audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/3 : observation {
    title "pdf-read surfaces about half the M1 inventory"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Updated after the pdf-read extension (Lege-ecosystem 09cf36e) and its consumption here (dd97c79).

        pdf-read now reports: open outcome with recovery events, the %PDF header with its leading-garbage offset, EncryptionInfo, XrefHealth, the trailer /ID pair, every /Info entry with a string value, XMP /Metadata presence, per-page PageStatus, annotation counts per /Subtype, a signature inventory (/FT /Sig fields walked through /Kids, each with /Filter, /SubFilter, /M, verbatim /ByteRange and whether that range spans the whole file), and seven FeatureFlags including has_embedded_files. Header and /ID are filled on the failed-open path too, since neither needs decryption — an unopenable encrypted document still yields its version, its garbage offset and its lineage identifiers.

        Still absent, and enumerated in DocumentFacts::pending in crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs: per-xref-section offsets, types and entry counts; linearization; the XMP packet's contents (it is detected but not parsed, so the xmpMM DocumentID, InstanceID, OriginalDocumentID, DerivedFrom and History lineage inside it is unread); per-annotation object identity; page representation classification; font and image inventories; and marked-content edit markers. Signature cryptography is deliberately out of scope until M4 — what exists is structural coverage only.

        The two shape findings from the previous revision still hold. pdf-read already implemented the structure-only path the encryption decision needs, via examine_failed. And the M1 plan's 'no new parsing work is required' is accurate but easy to misread: every fact added in 09cf36e was already reachable through the existing resolvers and simply unreported, so step 2 is surfacing work of real size rather than a thin assembly pass.

        The fixture gap is unchanged: the ecosystem has no document encrypted under a non-empty user password on a scheme the standard handler supports, so the password-upgrade half of the encrypted-mode-labelled acceptance check still cannot be tested. encrypted_fixture() opens with no password; aes256_encrypted_fixture() opens with none.
        """
    observed_at git:dd97c79d52ec5d653a3848b684430d060bbd7085
    method manual
    watches [ "crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs" ]
    blocks [ @audit.work.m1-plan ]
    supersedes [ @audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/2 ]
}

record audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/4 : observation {
    title "pdf-read surfaces most of the M1 inventory; the visual half remains"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Updated after the XMP work (Lege-ecosystem 1ea79e4) and its consumption here (c03bcc4).

        pdf-read now reports: open outcome with recovery events; the %PDF header with its leading-garbage offset; EncryptionInfo; XrefHealth; the trailer /ID pair; every /Info entry with a string value; XMP presence plus the xmpMM media-management lineage (DocumentID, InstanceID, OriginalDocumentID, the DerivedFrom document/instance pair, and the History event list with action, timestamp, software agent and changed-parts); per-page PageStatus; annotation counts per /Subtype; a signature inventory with verbatim /ByteRange and whole-file coverage; and seven FeatureFlags. Header and /ID are filled on the failed-open path, since neither needs decryption.

        Six sections remain pending, and they divide cleanly into two groups. Three are structural and cheap: per-xref-section offsets, types and entry counts (needs the XrefSection type from @audit.decision.revision-model, and is really M2 work); linearization; and per-annotation object identity (@lege.work.annotation-forensic-metadata). Three are visual or content-level and are the substance of the remaining M1 effort: page representation classification (Vector/RasterOnly/RasterWithTextLayer/Mixed/Blank/Unknown, needing per-page SemImage and SemFont counts, image coverage and effective DPI), font and image inventories, and marked-content edit markers (@audit.work.edit-provenance-facts). Signature cryptography is deliberately M4.

        The practical consequence: the checker can now establish document *lineage* — that one file descends from another, and what tool touched it when — but cannot yet say anything about what a page *looks like*. For the in-place-editing case that motivated @audit.work.edit-provenance-facts, the DocumentID/InstanceID comparison and the missing-signature-structure finding are reachable today; the /TouchUp_TextEdit markers and the intra-run font fragmentation are not.

        The XMP parser is a targeted scanner in pdf-read's xmp module, not a general XML parser. It matches on local names after the namespace prefix, which is deliberate — the xmpMM prefix is conventional rather than mandated — and accepts a theoretical collision with another namespace defining the same local name in exchange for working on files with non-standard prefixes.

        The fixture gap is unchanged: no fixture is encrypted under a non-empty user password on a scheme the standard handler supports, so the password-upgrade half of the encrypted-mode-labelled acceptance check still cannot be tested.
        """
    observed_at git:c03bcc495a3cdee94c8a6c178d18bcf7f9c4ead3
    method manual
    watches [ "crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs" ]
    blocks [ @audit.work.m1-plan ]
    supersedes [ @audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/3 ]
}

record audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/5 : observation {
    title "pdf-read surfaces the visual M1 inventory; edit markers remain"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Updated after the visual-fact work: pdf-read now compiles each page's SemanticPage into PageMetrics (text-run counts, visible vs invisible, path/shading paints, image coverage in crop-box basis points, effective DPI of the largest placement) plus document-level FontRecord and ImageRecord inventories. `embedded` on a font means the PDF carries a /FontFile* stream, not that the engine substituted a bundled Standard-14 face. pdf-forensics classifies each compiled page as Vector, RasterOnly, RasterWithTextLayer, Mixed, Blank, or Unknown, and those three former pending sections (page_representation, fonts, images) are gone from the report.

        pdf-read now reports: open outcome with recovery events; the %PDF header with its leading-garbage offset; EncryptionInfo; XrefHealth; the trailer /ID pair; every /Info entry with a string value; XMP presence plus the xmpMM media-management lineage; per-page PageStatus including representation metrics; annotation counts per /Subtype; a signature inventory with verbatim /ByteRange and whole-file coverage; seven FeatureFlags; and font and image inventories. Header and /ID are filled on the failed-open path, since neither needs decryption.

        Four sections remain pending. Three are structural and cheap: per-xref-section offsets, types and entry counts (needs the XrefSection type from @audit.decision.revision-model, and is really M2 work); linearization; and per-annotation object identity (@lege.work.annotation-forensic-metadata). One is the remaining M1 content-level gap: marked-content edit markers (@audit.work.edit-provenance-facts), which pdf-content currently keeps only for /OC and /ActualText and drops every other tag name, so /TouchUp_TextEdit is not recoverable without a lege-namespace accessor. Signature cryptography is deliberately M4.

        The practical consequence: the checker can now say both what lineage a file claims and what a page looks like — vector vs full-page raster vs raster-plus-invisible-text vs blank. For the in-place-editing case that motivated @audit.work.edit-provenance-facts, the DocumentID/InstanceID comparison, the missing-signature-structure finding, and a PDF-Verse-style flattened-export classification are reachable today; the /TouchUp_TextEdit markers and the intra-run font fragmentation are not.

        The XMP parser is a targeted scanner in pdf-read's xmp module, not a general XML parser. It matches on local names after the namespace prefix, which is deliberate — the xmpMM prefix is conventional rather than mandated — and accepts a theoretical collision with another namespace defining the same local name in exchange for working on files with non-standard prefixes.

        The fixture gap is unchanged: no fixture is encrypted under a non-empty user password on a scheme the standard handler supports, so the password-upgrade half of the encrypted-mode-labelled acceptance check still cannot be tested.

        pdf-integrity now lives at lege-pdf/pdf-integrity inside the Lege-ecosystem checkout as its own Cargo workspace, with path dependencies at ../render/crates/*.
        """
    observed_at git:03490dde438e7e86c0959d5d80c34a5faf2743f2
    method manual
    watches [
        "crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs",
        "crates/pdf-forensics/src/representation.rs",
        "crates/pdf-forensics/src/document.rs"
    ]
    blocks [ @audit.work.m1-plan ]
    supersedes [ @audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/4 ]
}

